Professor v1.2.3 is available. This chat updates this machine to it, working only in this Professor source clone. Never push, tag, publish or release, and never edit the source by hand.

1. Read what changed. Run `pfm version` for the installed version and `git fetch --tags origin`. Then read every release note after the installed version up to v1.2.3, oldest first, with `git show v1.2.3:releases/vX.Y.Z.md` (`git show v1.2.3:CHANGELOG.md` lists them). Skip none: a skipped note skips its migration steps. A note with a `#### → Stop:` line is a required stop: update to that release first, finish its steps, then continue from it.

2. Make one checklist from the notes' `#### → For:` actions. Each item says when it runs (`before update`, `after update` or `per project`) and what it touches; when two releases act on the same thing, the later one wins. An older action that names no timing, including one written `#### For:` or `#### → For adopters …:`, gets the timing you judge.

3. Ask before changing anything. Show the user a short, plain summary of what changes for them, with the checklist, then ask with AskUserQuestion whether to update now. On an engine without AskUserQuestion, ask the same question as the last block of your reply and wait for the answer.

4. Once the user says yes: do the `before update` actions, run `pfm update --to v1.2.3` (or the stop's tag), do the `after update` actions, then run `pfm doctor`.

5. Ask which projects to update. `pfm doctor` prints one `project_updates` warning per Professor project on this machine with open rows, naming its `root=` and its `unresolved=` count. Ask with AskUserQuestion, multi-select, one option per project: its folder name as the label, its path and open-row count as the description. A question holds four projects; put more projects in further questions of the same call. On an engine without AskUserQuestion, list the projects numbered as the last block of your reply and wait for the user's pick. With no project listed, skip to step 7. Update only the projects the user picks.

6. Update each picked project, one at a time. In its root, run `pfm doctor --project-updates` and take it to `clean`, exit 0. The user's pick covers every row, so no row waits for another question, and every row ends in a verb:
- `UPDATED`: port each listed upstream commit's intent into the local file, keeping the project's own edits, then `pfm update pin <local>`; a version kept on purpose is `pfm update pin <local> --keep "<what you did not take, and why>"`, never a row left open.
- `NEW`: decide from the project's own rules, then adopt it (`pfm update pin --template <template> <local>`) or `pfm update ignore <template>`.
- `RETIRED-NAME`: edit the line to its successor, or `pfm update accept <path> --reason "<why>"` for a mention that must stay.
- `INCOMPLETE`: inspect the unscanned text, then `pfm update accept <path> --coverage --reason "<why>"` once reviewed.
- `GONE-UPSTREAM`, `MOVED` and `LOCAL-DELETED`: the verbs printed on the row.
Run the verbs one at a time, never in parallel: each rewrites `.professor/baseline.json`. Then do the `per project` actions for that project, re-run the report and quote its last line.

7. End with a short report in plain words: the version now installed, the exact result of each step, each updated project's last report line, and each project not picked with the command that updates it later, `pfm doctor --project-updates --root <path>`.